A||||5||fierobob88||header for 88 4 cylindar||||1||
Z||000000||fierobob88||12-07-2001||02:07 PM||||Sup guys...I was thinking my 88 could really use a header...anyone have one on theirs and can give me a link where I could find one?  also let me know how it worked for you...thaks-Bob||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaveL||12-07-2001||03:55 PM||||I think a header on a 4 cylinder is a waste. You should just change it to a dual exhaust if it isn't already. If it is, cut off the converter and put back in a cherry bomb or something.<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ITB 2M4||12-08-2001||12:45 AM||||I've got one from Headman Headers, but they ain't cheap.  I'm running an '84 SE in the Sports Car Club of America's Improved Touring B class.  Sounds good, but not much hp increase at all.<BR>For the street, you'll have to really insulate it good from the starter.  It gets the starter solenoid hot, which won't engage the starter if you go into 7-11 for a minute.<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||14
Z||000003||Nashco||12-08-2001||12:48 AM||||Do you insulate the header or the starter??  It should be a lot more efficient and cheaper to insulate the starter with something similar to the fiberglass/metal foil heat shields used on V8 starters...right?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||12-08-2001||02:40 AM||||I will still say that the Hooker Tri-Y header was the best power increase for my 87 coupe. The stock exhaust manifold is a waste of welding. When I went to a straight pipe for a cat and stayed with a single tail pipe I was able to keep up with the stock V6s in my club running 225 wide tires. 1/4 mile times were improved as well. I'm still looking for my folder with my old time slips right now.<P>The flipside is that you will pay dearly with starters. Unless you reengineer that part of the pipe away from the solenoid you will fry it consistantly. I was doing a starter every 6 months. Im sure I made money on Pep Boys with their lifetime warranty on their starters. I put all sorts of shields, insulators and plates on the solenoid. At least on my header, it was just too close to do any good. <P>Wrapping the header would only rot it away.<P>Great idea. Just get ready to pay for the power.||||reg||14
Z||000005||qwikgta||12-08-2001||03:45 AM||||I have one on my 88 and it was the best thing I did to the little motor.  I noticed a diff in the 1-2 and 2-3 shift big time.  I have a straight pipe off the header to a supertrapp muff and it is great.  It is still a 2.5 so Im not trying to bluff anyone, it is still a dog but it is better than before, I also added an open air cleaner, it may or maynot do anything, but it did change the sound, to a deeper one.  And I did not do any heat mods to the starter in-fact, I removed all the factory heat sheilding down there and I rerouted the factory plastic duct for the intake to hit the starter since I didn't need it anymore fot the TBI.  I got my header on Ebay for 200.00 bucks and it was a deal in my opinion.<P>Good luck<P>Rob<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine2_4.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sig_Pic_copy.jpg"> ||||reg||1
